      "local_ref": "d1",
      "document_summary": "Academic article by Andreas G. Vlachopoulos (Aegis 
18, 2020) focused on the so‑called Ring of Nestor (CMS VI, no. 277). The paper 
reassesses earlier scholarly doubts and claims of forgery about the Ring of 
Nestor and related Creto‑Mycenaean gold rings, arguing that subsequent research 
and comparanda support their authenticity. It situates the ring within Late 
Bronze Age Aegean glyptic art (16th–15th century BC), emphasizes its large size 
and high craftsmanship, and analyzes its complex iconography. A key focus is the
reinterpretation of Arthur Evans’s description of a “watch‑dog” or “dragon” 
motif on the ring, which Vlachopoulos identifies instead as a scorpion 
functioning as a major apotropaic symbol. The paper connects this 
reinterpretation to broader patterns in Aegean iconography and uses technical 
study (including gold content and workmanship) and iconographic parallels 
(wall‑paintings, figurines, ivories, other rings) to substantiate authenticity 
and religious/iconographic significance.",
      "source_ref": "s1"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d2",
      "document_summary": "Doctoral thesis by Jennifer Linda Palmer (University 
of Birmingham, 2014) analyzing Late Bronze Age Aegean glyptic motifs on the 
Greek mainland and on Crete after Late Minoan IB, with an emphasis on 
religiously significant imagery. The thesis classifies and critically examines 
several glyptic themes, including anthropomorphic figures flanked by animals, 
seated women, figures with architecture, and animal sacrifice, in order to 
clarify their religious aspects and what they reveal about Late Bronze Age 
Aegean religious practices and the relationship between Minoan Crete and 
Mycenaean Greece. Within this broader framework, Palmer engages with the 
scholarly treatment of the Ring of Nestor (referencing work such as OJA 17 (1), 
1–13, \"Ring of Nestor\"), treating it as one of the key signet rings whose 
iconography has been assigned religious significance in scholarly literature and
using it among comparative examples in the discussion of religious motifs.",
      "source_ref": "s2"
    },
    {
